  "query_preview": "..The weak shortwave trough is now over northeast \nArizona this afternoon will continue to meander southeastward \nthrough Thursday. This will keeps the ongoing potential for high-\nbased and isolated convection, mainly for the eastern Arizona. \nThe trough is moving around a ridge that is southwest of northern\nArizona, which is close enough to provide us with above normal \ntemperatures for the next few days.\n\nBy the end of the week, the ridge moves a bit eastward and then \ncollapsing this weekend as a much stronger trough enters the \ninterior Pacific Northwest. This next trough will then dig south \nor southwestward, strengthening and possibly cutting off by Sunday\nor Monday. This will likely allow for significant tropical \nmoisture transport into Arizona, along with the remnants of \nHurricane Rachel. However, the GFS and ECMWF does not eject the \ntrough inland until next Tuesday or Wednesday, so this moisture \nstream could go on for awhile.\n\nThere are still many things that could change and shift this \nforecast. The Canadian ensemble has a faster eastward moving \ntrough, which phases better with the tropical moisture from \nHurricane Rachel moving into Arizona and is more favorable for \nmore widespread rainfall across all of Arizona Sunday into early\nWednesday then compared to the GFS and ECMWF ensembles. \nMeanwhile, the GFS and ECMWF ensembles have a slower moving \ntrough, likely related to a low cutting off within the trough by \nSunday or Monday. This favors the potential for heavier rainfall \nmore over western Arizona as moisture is pulled northward further \nwest, and shifting where the greatest plume of tropical moisture \nwill setup. There is still an level of uncertainty in the \nforecast that should be monitored closely. Nonetheless, this is \npotentially a very impactful pattern.\n\nFor now, there is higher confidence in a windy, colder and wet \npattern developing as early as Saturday and lasting well into next \nweek. There is still lower confidence in QPF amounts and the \npotential for intact tropical circulation remnants actually moving\ninto the state.",
